---
import Guide from '../layouts/Guide.astro'
import { doc, repo } from '../lib/content'
const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['summary', 'In short'],
  ['site', 'This website'],
  ['downloads', 'Download counts'],
  ['app', 'The app on your computer'],
  ['brain', 'Brain knowledge'],
  ['remote', 'Remote access and browser'],
  ['account', 'A Phosphor account'],
  ['services', 'Services we rely on'],
  ['delete', 'Deleting your data'],
  ['contact', 'Changes and questions'],
]
---

<Guide
  title="Privacy"
  eyebrow="Policy / last updated 15 November 2026"
  description="Phosphor is an open-source app that runs on your computer. This page lists everything the project's own servers receive, how long it is kept, and how to remove it."
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="summary">
    <h2>In short</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Your configured providers receive the prompts and tool results you send them. Connected
        services receive their requests. Phosphor’s control service never receives lane contents or
        provider keys; execution and saved sessions belong to your computers.
      </li><li>This website sets no cookies and runs no analytics or third-party scripts.</li><li>
        Download links add one to a daily total. Nothing about who clicked is stored.
      </li><li>
        A Phosphor account is optional. You only need one for remote access, and you can delete it
        yourself at any time.
      </li><li>
        Nothing is sold or shared for advertising. The source for every server is public in <a
          href={repo}>the repository</a
        >.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section id="site">
    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      phosphor.saccolabs.com is a set of static pages. It has no cookies, no tracking pixels and no
      client scripts. Tutorial video files and posters are served from this site, not an embedded
      third-party player. Playback begins only when you choose it; the pages include written
      walkthroughs. The server’s request log records time, a route category (static requests use
      site and a path class), response status and duration, rather than a full browsing URL. It
      doesn't record your IP address, and the log is kept only as long as the server's log rotation
      keeps it.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="downloads">
    <h2>Download counts</h2>
    <p>
      The site's download links and install command go through <code>/download/…</code> on this server.
      It adds one to a total for that day and that link, then sends you to the GitHub release. The stored
      row is the date, which link, and the count. No address, cookie, referrer or browser details are
      stored with it. These aggregate totals have no scheduled expiry.
    </p>
    <p>
      To stop one visitor inflating the totals, the server keeps a keyed hash of the address in
      memory for up to an hour. The key is random, made fresh every time the server starts, and
      never written down, so the hash can't be turned back into an address. Linking to GitHub
      directly skips the count entirely.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="app">
    <h2>The app on your computer</h2>
    <p>
      Phosphor Desktop and the Host run <a href="https://pi.dev">pi</a>, which talks to the model
      providers you set up. Those requests go straight from your computer to the provider, under the
      provider's own terms. The control service does not proxy or store them. Local pi session files
      and app diagnostics remain on the execution machine; review them for sensitive data before
      sharing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Desktop checks GitHub Releases for updates, so GitHub sees that request. Phosphor's servers
      don't.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="brain">
    <h2>Brain knowledge</h2>
    <p>
      Brain is optional, off by default and Desktop-only. The knowledge graph, proposals, evidence
      and embeddings stay in local app storage. The local embedding model is downloaded when you
      enable Brain.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a lane you explicitly queue, reading sends scrubbed user and assistant text to the reader
      model in tool-less calls. It can consume tokens and quota. Scrubbing is not a guarantee that
      every sensitive detail is removed. With recall on, kept knowledge can become context in later
      provider requests.
    </p>
    <p>
      Defrag runs only when you click it. Defrag sends what waits, including memories, names and
      scrubbed excerpts, to the Brain lane's model. Brain Agent may read the connectors that lane
      has to check facts. Lane agents can flag a memory for Brain Agent to check during Defrag.
    </p>
    <p>
      Review before saving is on by default. Forget removes an individual memory and unused
      evidence; Reset Brain knowledge removes the graph and extraction records while keeping
      settings and the model. Turning Brain off keeps existing knowledge. It does not sync to Hosts
      or other computers. <a href="/guides/brain/#privacy">Retention and controls</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="remote">
    <h2>Remote access and the browser</h2>
    <p>
      Desktop and the PWA connect directly to your Host over private Tailscale HTTPS/WSS. The Host
      owns files, transcripts, artifacts and provider credentials. Control handles identity and
      grants, not a relay of lane contents.
    </p>
    <p>
      Desktop keeps remote content in memory rather than caching it on disk; explicit exports save
      where you choose. Desktop lane moves carry a private temporary snapshot between machines, not
      credentials. The browser stores its sign-in cookie and app shell, not an offline transcript
      archive. <a href={doc('remote-access.md#trust-boundary')}>Remote trust boundary</a> · <a
        href={doc('web-client.md')}>Browser storage and capabilities</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="account">
    <h2>A Phosphor account</h2>
    <p>
      You only need an account to reach your own machines remotely. Signing in with GitHub creates
      it. The control server then keeps:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>your GitHub login, user id, name, avatar and email, as GitHub provides them;</li><li>
        each machine you connect: its label, hostname, platform, Phosphor version, public key, when
        it was last seen, and the number of lanes running (never what is in them);
      </li><li>the apps and browsers you've authorized, and when they were authorized;</li><li>
        a log of connecting, renaming and removing machines, kept for 180 days, without labels or
        addresses.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The request log notes your account id against each signed-in request. To rate-limit sign-in
      and API calls, the server holds your address in memory for up to an hour. It is never written
      to disk.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href="https://phosphor-app.saccolabs.com/">web app</a> keeps you signed in with an encrypted
      first-party session cookie and uses a temporary first-party transaction cookie during sign-in. Neither
      is for advertising or analytics. The details are in the <a
        href={doc('remote-access.md#trust-boundary')}>remote access trust boundary</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="services">
    <h2>Services we rely on</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Cloudflare</strong> sits in front of both sites and sees your IP address, as any network
        provider does.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Supabase</strong> hosts the account database and sign-in. Its sign-in records can include
        your IP address and browser.
      </li><li>
        <strong>GitHub</strong> handles sign-in and hosts the downloads.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each one handles that data under its own privacy policy.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="delete">
    <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
    <p>
      Deleting your account removes it from Phosphor's database, along with your machines,
      authorized apps and their log. Any connected Host stops on its next check-in. Nothing on your
      own computers is touched. Request logs and the deletion event can remain under log rotation;
      this is not an immediate erasure guarantee for logs or service backups. Download counts aren't
      linked to anyone, so there's nothing in them to remove.
    </p>
    <p>
      Without an account, there is no Phosphor account record to delete; website request logs and
      aggregate download counts described above still apply. Uninstalling the app does not promise
      to erase local settings, pi sessions or Brain knowledge. Use the in-app Brain controls and
      remove any local data you no longer want separately.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="contact">
    <h2>Changes and questions</h2>
    <p>
      If this changes, this page changes in the same update, and the <a
        href={`${repo}/commits/main/apps/site/src/pages/privacy.astro`}>history of this page</a
      > is public. For questions, <a href={`${repo}/issues`}>open an issue</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
